course: dictionary
lesson: 7935
title: Really: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Really» is a small word that can change how a sentence feels. Look at the words around it, the context, to work out which meaning it has. «Is it really true?» asks if something is true, so really means in fact or truly.
explain: «It was really hot on the beach.» Here really means very. Try swapping in another word: «It was very hot on the beach» still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning. Check your idea by putting it back into the sentence.
explain: Really can also soften a statement. «I don't really like it» is gentler than «I don't like it», because really makes the no less harsh. In books, «Really?» shows that a character is surprised. In «Really, Tom?», the voice and the tone tell you the speaker is disappointed.
fact: «Really» can mean in fact or truly, as in «Is it really true?»
fact: «Really» can mean very, as in «really hot».
fact: «Really?» often shows surprise or doubt.
fact: «Really» can soften or strengthen a statement, as in «I don't really like it.»
fact: «Really, Tom?» means I am disappointed in you, while «Oh, really?» means I don't believe you.
exercise: «Is it really true?» What does really mean in this sentence? | Quickly | Very cold | *In fact, truly | Far away
exercise: «It was really hot on the beach.» What does really mean here? | Quietly | In fact | Never | *Very
exercise: A character says «Really?» after hearing big news. What does this show? | Boredom | *Surprise | Hunger | Sleepiness
test: «I don't really like it.» What does really do in this sentence? | *It makes the statement softer | It turns the sentence into a question | It makes the word like mean the opposite | It means the speaker is very hungry
test: Mum sighs and says, «Really, Tom?» What does she mean? | *She is disappointed in you | She wants a biscuit | She is very happy with you | She is feeling cold
exam: In a school report you want a formal word instead of really in «The new library is really large.» Which word fits best? | quietly | *exceedingly | yesterday | mid
